CREATED:20260407T145901Z
DESCRIPTION:Thousands of negatives preserved at the Library of Congress we
 re\nperforated to render them unusable: these are the so-called killed\nn
 egatives. This practice of disposal was carried out by the director of th
 e\nphotographic section of the Farm Security Administration\, created to\
 ndocument government aid during one of the most devastating periods of\ne
 cological and human crisis in United States history. Ninety years later\,
 \nin the midst of an unprecedented planetary crisis\, how does this archi
 ve\naddress us? Which readings\, previously ignored\, now gain force? Can
 \nthese "killed" images be revived through artistic practices that find i
 n\nthem a critical contemporary sensibility?\nThe research presented at D
 uke University is grounded in an\necofeminist methodology\, drawing on th
 e theory of terricide developed\nby Mapuche Indigenous leader Moira Milán
  and on the concept of strong\nrationality proposed by Colombian anthropo
 logist Arturo Escobar\, both\ncentered on interdependence with the enviro
 nment. It takes Ground\n(2019) by Bill McDowell\, in which black circles 
 shift from marks of\nrejection to aesthetic forms and openings for interp
 retation. The hole\nthus operates as a creative force and a temporal aper
 ture\, reactivating\nthe relational tension with the earth as a shared an
 d ongoing problem.
DURATION:PT1H30M
DTSTAMP:20260407T151533Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260414T163000
LAST-MODIFIED:20260407T151533Z
LOCATION;X-BEDEWORK-UID=18832edc-1b27e154-011b-282c2295-00000048:Languages
  305
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:“Agrietar el Archivo. Imagen\, Tierra y Crisis en los Killed Negat
 ives”
